1
00:00:00,351 --> 00:00:04,232
[SPEAKER_01]: From the Anguttara Nikaya, a sutta called Walking.

2
00:00:05,213 --> 00:00:05,673
[SPEAKER_02]: Walking?

3
00:00:05,813 --> 00:00:07,413
[SPEAKER_01]: Walking.

4
00:00:07,433 --> 00:00:27,040
[SPEAKER_01]: And it just says, if a sensual thought or thought of ill-will or cruelty arises while walking, and if you tolerate it and do not abandon it, then you are devoid of effort and moral shame, constantly lazy and lacking energy while walking.

5
00:00:31,052 --> 00:00:48,235
[SPEAKER_01]: And it goes on, whether walking, standing, sitting or lying down, one who thinks bad thoughts, connected with household life, has entered upon the wrong path, infatuated by delusive things, such a person cannot be free.

6
00:00:49,095 --> 00:00:56,537
[SPEAKER_01]: But one who has his thoughts calmed and delights in stilling of thought, such a person can be free.

7
00:00:58,097 --> 00:01:08,904
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's a lot of the walking meditation out there people like to do, but it's... they focus on the the feeling of their feet on the sand.

8
00:01:09,104 --> 00:01:09,645
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah.

9
00:01:09,665 --> 00:01:11,126
[SPEAKER_02]: Not the actual nature of the thinking.

10
00:01:11,146 --> 00:01:12,006
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's quite common.

11
00:01:12,266 --> 00:01:14,528
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's, you know, walk from this point to that point.

12
00:01:15,028 --> 00:01:15,709
[SPEAKER_01]: How many steps?

13
00:01:15,829 --> 00:01:16,349
[SPEAKER_01]: 30 steps?

14
00:01:16,369 --> 00:01:18,250
[SPEAKER_02]: Same for anybody posture, when they're sitting.

15
00:01:18,471 --> 00:01:23,594
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, when the Buddha says he's upon the wrong path, means he's going down the wrong direction.

16
00:01:24,891 --> 00:01:27,572
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what it means in a more, in a more, most accurate manner.

17
00:01:28,412 --> 00:01:31,493
[SPEAKER_02]: So, going down the wrong path, it's basically attending to things wrongly.

18
00:01:31,914 --> 00:01:36,035
[SPEAKER_02]: That means going down the wrong direction of your, of your efforts.

19
00:01:36,675 --> 00:01:38,056
[SPEAKER_02]: So, even the ones who are making the efforts.

20
00:01:38,076 --> 00:01:39,536
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's exactly the example you gave.

21
00:01:39,576 --> 00:01:51,761
[SPEAKER_02]: When, eh, when you're doing walking meditation, mostly today people, again, they're after, you're after experience of peace or some, some sort of, some sort of, eh...

22
00:01:57,756 --> 00:02:11,267
[SPEAKER_02]: You're after experience of peace, or some sort of experience, or some novelty, or the other, but not many people would use the meditation to reflect upon the nature of the thoughts.

23
00:02:12,087 --> 00:02:24,417
[SPEAKER_02]: Yet, that's all this is to talk about, as anything that really matters, is to not entertain the unwholesome, and to cultivate the wholesome, whether you're sitting, walking, lying down, or standing.

24
00:02:25,179 --> 00:02:28,380
[SPEAKER_02]: Whether you're talking, whether you're alone or with people.

25
00:02:28,780 --> 00:02:44,783
[SPEAKER_02]: But, yes, unfortunately, today, you get this, like a mass group retreat centers and so on, and then you get taught, like, walking meditation, you observe the soles of your feet and you try to, like, be as present as possible and catch the moment and so on.

26
00:02:45,523 --> 00:02:53,285
[SPEAKER_02]: All that is nothing other than just a focusing practice on a particular aspect of your body, on a particular, basically, type of perception, of touch.

27
00:02:54,145 --> 00:02:54,505
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

28
00:02:55,246 --> 00:03:11,220
[SPEAKER_02]: And it can be so... it can... it can produce a release for people, because most of the... most of the... rest of the... rest of the time, they spend... they spend their... their day in an unmindful state, very busy, very engaged, and then just focusing on one particular thing and refusing to give attention to everything else.

29
00:03:11,860 --> 00:03:14,903
[SPEAKER_02]: Seems like a way to go, because it provides you with a degree of peace.

30
00:03:15,463 --> 00:03:18,265
[SPEAKER_02]: But that peace is only relative to your lifestyle beforehand.

31
00:03:19,026 --> 00:03:23,627
[SPEAKER_02]: That peace is not the measure of your understanding and wisdom that comes if you do the dhamma correctly.

32
00:03:24,467 --> 00:03:27,188
[SPEAKER_02]: And it's not a hard thing to see that.

33
00:03:27,268 --> 00:03:44,812
[SPEAKER_02]: Just see when you're out of the retreat, how much of your composure you can keep when you go back to your daily life, to your other things, dealing with things, whether you'll still be able to remain impenetrable to the unwholesome states as you're walking, sitting, lying down or standing.

34
00:03:46,270 --> 00:03:47,171
[SPEAKER_02]: And most people won't.

35
00:03:47,211 --> 00:03:53,338
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why they have to keep going back for retreats, prolonging retreats, doing longer retreats, more intense retreats, all that.

36
00:03:53,498 --> 00:04:01,667
[SPEAKER_01]: But then they say, if I focus on my feet, walking on the sand, then I don't think any sensual thoughts.

37
00:04:01,687 --> 00:04:03,549
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, that's the thing, that's the thing.

38
00:04:05,891 --> 00:04:08,234
[SPEAKER_02]: Where you focus is not the root of the problem.

39
00:04:10,881 --> 00:04:16,484
[SPEAKER_02]: The root of the problem is not understanding the nature of unwholesome, and because of that, being subject to it.

40
00:04:17,204 --> 00:04:22,287
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you don't know how, even unwittingly, you entertain it, you fuel it, you feed it.

41
00:04:23,647 --> 00:04:25,128
[SPEAKER_02]: Which means you end up acting out of it.

42
00:04:26,369 --> 00:04:29,150
[SPEAKER_02]: So, think of unwholesome thoughts as a problem.

43
00:04:30,108 --> 00:04:32,911
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, proliferating unwholesome thoughts are a further problem.

44
00:04:33,531 --> 00:04:42,999
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what happens when people do the focusing practice, they would focus on one particular thing and refuse to give attention to everything else, including the problem, the problem of unwholesome.

45
00:04:44,321 --> 00:04:46,503
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then they can feel like, yeah, this is really working.

46
00:04:46,523 --> 00:04:56,812
[SPEAKER_02]: And, I mean, I'm not saying there's absolutely, like, no benefit whatsoever, but I'm saying the benefit is only relative for those who are distracted most of the other time.

47
00:04:58,070 --> 00:05:03,614
[SPEAKER_02]: Distracted with duty, with family, with sensuality, or just with your views, in some cases.

48
00:05:04,314 --> 00:05:10,258
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the point is to recognize that, like, yeah, not giving attention to the problem can help you, but that's not solving the problem.

49
00:05:10,678 --> 00:05:13,120
[SPEAKER_02]: That's just managing it, dealing with it temporarily.

50
00:05:13,620 --> 00:05:14,481
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's exactly the point.

51
00:05:14,801 --> 00:05:20,925
[SPEAKER_02]: You can practice and further this type of focusing meditation only in such retreat settings.

52
00:05:21,445 --> 00:05:30,089
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't sustain that out in the world, or you can't sustain that if you have to climb up and down the mountain every single day for Pindapar for five kilometers in rain or whatever else.

53
00:05:30,589 --> 00:05:32,710
[SPEAKER_02]: You can try, but you're going to burn out very soon.

54
00:05:33,810 --> 00:05:46,075
[SPEAKER_02]: As you see many, many, many cases that, well, we both kind of witnessed, with the monks who would go in the great seclusion, focused on some sort of technique to do it, and then just after a week or two, you can't handle it anymore.

55
00:05:47,806 --> 00:05:48,786
[SPEAKER_02]: You need something.

56
00:05:48,806 --> 00:05:52,388
[SPEAKER_02]: You need some reference point, because, okay, now you stopped attending.

57
00:05:52,768 --> 00:05:54,548
[SPEAKER_02]: You're just focusing on one thing, but that's it.

58
00:05:54,828 --> 00:05:55,889
[SPEAKER_02]: That's a wall.

59
00:05:55,909 --> 00:05:56,509
[SPEAKER_02]: That's a dead end.

60
00:05:57,109 --> 00:06:05,992
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the only benefit you were getting before, from your meditation practice, wasn't because of what you were focusing on, it's because of what you were not focusing on, which was the unwholesome.

61
00:06:06,792 --> 00:06:08,332
[SPEAKER_02]: You were not focusing on unwholesome.

62
00:06:08,672 --> 00:06:09,993
[SPEAKER_02]: That was the peace you were getting.

63
00:06:11,003 --> 00:06:21,850
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, you arrived at not focusing on unwholesome, because you're focusing on something else, which sooner or later, environment will prevent you to do so, or you're just going to run out of the of the most obvious inspiration to do so.

64
00:06:22,291 --> 00:06:23,191
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, what you're going to do?

65
00:06:23,551 --> 00:06:31,697
[SPEAKER_02]: You're just back in the same unwholesome, which remain intact, because you haven't been understanding it, discerning it, resolving it.

66
00:06:31,917 --> 00:06:37,441
[SPEAKER_02]: You've been ignoring it, through watching the soles of your feet, through watching the tip of your nose, and so on.

67
00:06:38,503 --> 00:06:39,123
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's what I mean.

68
00:06:39,764 --> 00:06:44,247
[SPEAKER_02]: Nobody denies that people can experience peaceful feelings when they do focusing meditation.

69
00:06:44,907 --> 00:06:47,849
[SPEAKER_02]: But the point here is that won't result in uprooting the problem.

70
00:06:48,590 --> 00:06:49,590
[SPEAKER_02]: It's only covering it up.

71
00:06:51,232 --> 00:06:56,075
[SPEAKER_02]: You can only approve the problem if you start looking at it for what it is, directly.

72
00:06:57,236 --> 00:07:00,758
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's exactly what the part of the sutta you read just talks about.

73
00:07:01,993 --> 00:07:03,174
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's what you recognize.

74
00:07:03,194 --> 00:07:10,160
[SPEAKER_02]: The work is not about me doing walking meditation for eight hours a day, or focusing on my nostrils all the time.

75
00:07:10,180 --> 00:07:19,808
[SPEAKER_02]: The work is recognizing whether the mind is affected by ill-will, affected by greed, affected by delusion, affected by unwholesome states, and then not entertaining that.

76
00:07:20,429 --> 00:07:24,812
[SPEAKER_02]: Doesn't matter whether I'm walking, sitting, lying down, or standing.

77
00:07:24,832 --> 00:07:25,573
[SPEAKER_01]: What posture?

78
00:07:26,297 --> 00:07:31,820
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, the posture is your reference point that you don't need to interfere with, if you know where the problem is.

79
00:07:32,100 --> 00:07:34,882
[SPEAKER_02]: And the problem is mind affected by unwholesome.

80
00:07:36,303 --> 00:07:42,106
[SPEAKER_02]: So, indeed, sometimes, when it's just too much to deal with, yeah, just ignore it.

81
00:07:42,306 --> 00:07:46,829
[SPEAKER_02]: Just don't pay any attention to it until it calms down, so that you can go back to it and deal with it.

82
00:07:48,730 --> 00:07:48,850
[SPEAKER_02]: But

83
00:07:50,380 --> 00:08:03,965
[SPEAKER_02]: Adopting a practice of just constantly not looking at the problem and focusing on something else, as a way of release from the problem, it's not going to resolve anything, because there's absolutely no discernment there, no wisdom, no understanding, because you're not looking at it.

84
00:08:04,385 --> 00:08:04,925
[SPEAKER_02]: Distraction.

85
00:08:05,586 --> 00:08:06,646
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a form of distraction.

86
00:08:06,986 --> 00:08:10,608
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why, that's why the Buddha did say, when he gave that simile, he says,

87
00:08:13,657 --> 00:08:22,901
[SPEAKER_02]: Not being free... not being free from sensuality, these samanas and brāhmaṇas, they pre-meditate, mis-meditate, over-meditate, under-meditate, but they don't meditate.

88
00:08:23,642 --> 00:08:24,602
[SPEAKER_02]: And it gives a simile.

89
00:08:25,022 --> 00:08:32,886
[SPEAKER_02]: It says they are like... like an owl hunting for a mouse, or a cat waiting to pounce on a fish, or... or... or... or... similes.

90
00:08:32,906 --> 00:08:39,869
[SPEAKER_02]: Basically, similes of preying upon something, of focusing on one moment where you act, where you catch it, where you grab it.

91
00:08:41,492 --> 00:08:44,014
[SPEAKER_02]: And he said, that's meditating with sensuality.

92
00:08:44,414 --> 00:08:49,397
[SPEAKER_02]: That's looking completely down the wrong path.

93
00:08:49,557 --> 00:08:54,541
[SPEAKER_02]: So that's, again, just imagine yourself preying upon something.

94
00:08:54,561 --> 00:08:57,502
[SPEAKER_02]: You're waiting for, I don't know, a mouse to come out of this hole.

95
00:08:58,103 --> 00:09:01,285
[SPEAKER_02]: You mustn't skip a beat, because he might escape you.

96
00:09:02,470 --> 00:09:15,375
[SPEAKER_02]: And see whether that's different from when people try to not skip a beat when they're observing the soles of their feet, or not skip a beat when they're focusing on the nostrils, or the belly movement, or whatever.

97
00:09:15,395 --> 00:09:18,816
[SPEAKER_02]: You end up cultivating the same attitude of preying upon something.

98
00:09:18,956 --> 00:09:22,138
[SPEAKER_02]: Preying upon something that you clearly think has value.

99
00:09:22,818 --> 00:09:24,178
[SPEAKER_02]: It's going to resolve all your problems.

100
00:09:24,238 --> 00:09:25,899
[SPEAKER_02]: Catching this mouse will fix all my problems.

101
00:09:26,279 --> 00:09:30,821
[SPEAKER_02]: Catching these perfect moments that come and go in front of me would purify my mind.

102
00:09:32,215 --> 00:09:33,095
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's a completely...

103
00:09:33,115 --> 00:09:35,637
[SPEAKER_02]: He said people who meditate like that, meditate with sensuality.

104
00:09:36,237 --> 00:09:40,899
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why they pre-meditate, miss-meditate, over-meditate, under-meditate and so on, but they don't meditate.

105
00:09:41,999 --> 00:09:58,027
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as you walk in, a thought of cruelty arises, and they don't tolerate it, which means... Yeah, which means you don't... like, thought of cruelty arises, means there is your work there for you, in its entirety.

106
00:09:59,788 --> 00:10:00,568
[SPEAKER_02]: Thought of cruelty.

107
00:10:01,611 --> 00:10:02,232
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the problem.

108
00:10:02,272 --> 00:10:02,772
[SPEAKER_02]: Nothing else.

109
00:10:03,993 --> 00:10:06,395
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you don't... you don't stop everything you've been doing.

110
00:10:06,415 --> 00:10:10,679
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't... you... like... just give it all up, because I will never...

111
00:10:10,879 --> 00:10:12,740
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't want ever to have such thoughts again.

112
00:10:13,401 --> 00:10:15,643
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't do that, because... well, it's not up to you.

113
00:10:15,803 --> 00:10:17,304
[SPEAKER_02]: Those thoughts will be presented themselves.

114
00:10:17,704 --> 00:10:26,292
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in order for you to not be victim of those presentations of ill-will or sensuality, you need to understand how you engage with it.

115
00:10:27,078 --> 00:10:36,322
[SPEAKER_02]: And you won't understand the habits of your engagement with those thoughts, while they're simultaneously present there, if your soul responds to that, is look away.

116
00:10:37,443 --> 00:10:38,423
[SPEAKER_02]: It's... just look away.

117
00:10:38,663 --> 00:10:39,283
[SPEAKER_02]: Just ignore it.

118
00:10:39,364 --> 00:10:40,724
[SPEAKER_02]: Just... just concentrate on my breath.

119
00:10:40,764 --> 00:10:41,424
[SPEAKER_02]: Concentrate on my breath.

120
00:10:41,544 --> 00:10:42,185
[SPEAKER_02]: Stop thinking about it.

121
00:10:42,205 --> 00:10:42,905
[SPEAKER_02]: Stop thinking about it.

122
00:10:44,366 --> 00:10:53,590
[SPEAKER_02]: And on top of that, you're going to wind yourself up much more, because thinking that you must stop thinking, is another thought, that you're thinking, in order to not think.

123
00:10:55,364 --> 00:11:02,233
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why it becomes so intense and sort of unpleasant.

124
00:11:03,054 --> 00:11:05,637
[SPEAKER_02]: You recognize the thought of cruelty.

125
00:11:06,298 --> 00:11:07,299
[SPEAKER_02]: In a way, that's enough.

126
00:11:07,800 --> 00:11:08,361
[SPEAKER_02]: Very often.

127
00:11:08,441 --> 00:11:11,365
[SPEAKER_02]: Sometimes, your recognition might not be clear enough.

128
00:11:12,388 --> 00:11:13,529
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then you need to endure it.

129
00:11:14,389 --> 00:11:27,895
[SPEAKER_02]: You have to recognize whereby... how much of the thought arises on its own, and then you have to recognize where the... where the line is, where if you cross, then it's you doing it, you entertaining it, you getting involved with it, you trying to get rid of it.

130
00:11:29,296 --> 00:11:31,657
[SPEAKER_02]: All of those are ways of fueling the thought.

131
00:11:33,660 --> 00:11:36,441
[SPEAKER_02]: Including you actively trying to ignore it.

132
00:11:37,041 --> 00:11:38,961
[SPEAKER_02]: Why would... like, there is a thought in your mind.

133
00:11:38,981 --> 00:11:40,642
[SPEAKER_02]: I must focus on my soles of my feet.

134
00:11:40,762 --> 00:11:41,002
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

135
00:11:41,642 --> 00:11:46,383
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you're trying to avoid the arisen thought in your mind that you think you must not have.

136
00:11:48,204 --> 00:11:54,646
[SPEAKER_02]: And by trying to avoid things that are disagreeable to you, what are you doing?

137
00:11:54,666 --> 00:11:56,866
[SPEAKER_02]: You're doing exactly the thing you shouldn't be doing.

138
00:11:56,946 --> 00:11:59,047
[SPEAKER_02]: Acting out of craving in regard to how you're feeling.

139
00:11:59,927 --> 00:12:01,087
[SPEAKER_02]: There is the unpleasant feeling.

140
00:12:01,268 --> 00:12:01,768
[SPEAKER_02]: Get rid of it.

141
00:12:01,868 --> 00:12:02,348
[SPEAKER_02]: Get rid of it.

142
00:12:02,448 --> 00:12:02,928
[SPEAKER_02]: Get rid of it.

143
00:12:04,275 --> 00:12:05,416
[SPEAKER_02]: There is the pleasant feeling.

144
00:12:05,796 --> 00:12:06,576
[SPEAKER_02]: I must have it longer.

145
00:12:06,616 --> 00:12:07,477
[SPEAKER_01]: I must have it longer.

146
00:12:07,577 --> 00:12:11,079
[SPEAKER_01]: There's the recognition that this is a cruel thought.

147
00:12:11,919 --> 00:12:12,560
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

148
00:12:12,580 --> 00:12:13,560
[SPEAKER_02]: That's not your doing.

149
00:12:14,321 --> 00:12:16,042
[SPEAKER_02]: The cruel thought presented itself.

150
00:12:16,262 --> 00:12:17,463
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a cruel thought.

151
00:12:17,523 --> 00:12:18,143
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not mine.

152
00:12:18,223 --> 00:12:18,904
[SPEAKER_02]: This is a cruel thought.

153
00:12:18,944 --> 00:12:20,965
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like somebody comes right here in front of your door.

154
00:12:22,265 --> 00:12:23,346
[SPEAKER_02]: And this is a public space.

155
00:12:23,986 --> 00:12:24,847
[SPEAKER_02]: Your kuti is yours.

156
00:12:25,007 --> 00:12:25,728
[SPEAKER_02]: They can't come in.

157
00:12:26,208 --> 00:12:27,929
[SPEAKER_02]: But they have every right to stand in front of your kuti.

158
00:12:28,589 --> 00:12:31,371
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you feel affected by that, that's on you.

159
00:12:32,612 --> 00:12:39,434
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you come out of your kuti and start to chase them, well, now you actually went into something that's not your domain, not your ownership, and trying to control it.

160
00:12:40,914 --> 00:12:43,735
[SPEAKER_02]: Which then means you left kuti unattended, and then other things can come in.

161
00:12:45,095 --> 00:12:46,755
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you're everywhere and nowhere.

162
00:12:48,056 --> 00:12:50,176
[SPEAKER_00]: And the same, in the same manner, the thing cannot come in.

163
00:12:51,336 --> 00:12:54,297
[SPEAKER_02]: Whatever thought is, unless you invite it in, unless you drag it in.

164
00:12:55,528 --> 00:12:56,869
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's what happens with sensuality.

165
00:12:57,829 --> 00:13:04,794
[SPEAKER_02]: Either sensuality, as in like agreeable desire, or disagreeable, something you want to get rid of, it just comes in front of you.

166
00:13:06,595 --> 00:13:08,776
[SPEAKER_02]: But if it's agreeable, you are the one who brings it in.

167
00:13:09,217 --> 00:13:11,838
[SPEAKER_02]: If it's disagreeable, you are the one who goes out and tries to chase it.

168
00:13:12,399 --> 00:13:15,180
[SPEAKER_02]: Either way, you overstep your boundary.

169
00:13:15,721 --> 00:13:17,542
[SPEAKER_02]: And because of that, you're subjected to it.

170
00:13:17,582 --> 00:13:18,903
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why you're affected by it.

171
00:13:23,031 --> 00:13:41,505
[SPEAKER_02]: So, there's that another sutta where the Buddha did say, while he was still unenlightened bodhisattva, when he was in the... it's called fear and dread, nājā-mikāya, where he said you'd be staying in this frightening forest and groves with wild animals, and then you'd be doing walking meditation at night.

172
00:13:42,346 --> 00:13:47,310
[SPEAKER_02]: And he said if there is a... if you would hear some sound or something, or have a frightening thought,

173
00:13:49,483 --> 00:13:53,466
[SPEAKER_02]: He wouldn't change what he was doing, which is walking with it.

174
00:13:53,486 --> 00:13:54,347
[SPEAKER_02]: He wouldn't stop walking.

175
00:13:55,908 --> 00:14:00,292
[SPEAKER_02]: He wouldn't stop walking until he dispels that unwholesome state of mind.

176
00:14:01,473 --> 00:14:03,634
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, he might change the posture.

177
00:14:03,674 --> 00:14:15,244
[SPEAKER_02]: In the same manner, whether he was seated, standing, sitting or lying down, he wouldn't change the posture before... unless he first removed the unwholesome state that had arisen.

178
00:14:16,144 --> 00:14:17,626
[SPEAKER_02]: And you remove the unwholesome state,

179
00:14:18,654 --> 00:14:19,675
[SPEAKER_02]: By not acting out of it.

180
00:14:20,736 --> 00:14:21,856
[SPEAKER_02]: By not trying to get rid of it.

181
00:14:21,996 --> 00:14:23,938
[SPEAKER_02]: By not trying to prolong it.

182
00:14:23,978 --> 00:14:24,819
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's exactly what it means.

183
00:14:25,239 --> 00:14:29,462
[SPEAKER_02]: I wouldn't change the posture until this state is dispelled.

184
00:14:30,663 --> 00:14:37,708
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if the unwholesome state came up, and now he stopped walking and done something else, means he acted out of it.

185
00:14:38,108 --> 00:14:40,470
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's already a way of fueling it.

186
00:14:41,837 --> 00:14:55,720
[SPEAKER_02]: Whether you acted out of it in a form of trying to prolong it, as in an unwholesome state of sensuality, or trying to deny it, get rid of it as quickly as possible, as in an unwholesome state of ill-will, or fear, or anything disagreeable.

187
00:14:57,400 --> 00:15:05,862
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the point is, you recognize, and then, how much of it arises on its own, and then, you don't either add to it, or take away from it.

188
00:15:07,142 --> 00:15:09,383
[SPEAKER_02]: And when it subsides, then you change the posture.

189
00:15:12,289 --> 00:15:13,570
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you do that long enough?

190
00:15:14,251 --> 00:15:22,397
[SPEAKER_02]: If you do that long enough, any... no matter how subtle ways of fueling the unwholesome states of your mind you've been entertaining, they will all fade.

191
00:15:23,017 --> 00:15:25,759
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you just... you're uprooting it left and right.

192
00:15:26,840 --> 00:15:27,921
[SPEAKER_02]: Bit by bit by bit.

193
00:15:28,581 --> 00:15:32,344
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, you can uproot things, but it takes time for them to sort of wither and die.

194
00:15:34,226 --> 00:15:39,470
[SPEAKER_02]: But one thing for sure, they'll never wither and die, and you certainly never uprooted them, if you keep looking elsewhere.

195
00:15:41,483 --> 00:15:54,365
[SPEAKER_02]: If you keep focusing on your nostrils for the sake of avoiding the thought and not having to think about anything and not having to experience displeasure, if you keep focusing on the soles of your feet because you're trying to catch a perfect moment and develop this ultimate peace, they will never be disturbed.

196
00:15:57,546 --> 00:16:02,207
[SPEAKER_02]: The non-disturbance doesn't come from not experiencing disturbing things.

197
00:16:03,567 --> 00:16:08,408
[SPEAKER_02]: Non-disturbance comes from not being disturbed by the disturbing things.

198
00:16:10,282 --> 00:16:16,677
[SPEAKER_02]: So, for as long as you're concerned with disturbing things, not with your response in regard to disturbing things, you're looking in the wrong direction.

199
00:16:16,838 --> 00:16:18,020
[SPEAKER_02]: You are down the wrong path.

200
00:16:32,906 --> 00:16:34,287
[SPEAKER_02]: Something to do, basically.

201
00:16:34,747 --> 00:16:41,951
[SPEAKER_02]: You're focused on doing things, and staring at things, and perceiving things, and observing things, but not looking your attitude behind that.

202
00:16:42,771 --> 00:16:46,833
[SPEAKER_02]: The intentionality behind, the motivation behind, the craving behind.

203
00:16:47,453 --> 00:16:48,634
[SPEAKER_02]: Craving is always behind.

204
00:16:48,954 --> 00:16:50,175
[SPEAKER_02]: It's always in regard to.

205
00:16:51,035 --> 00:16:54,678
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you never look in that direction of that background, you're never going to see it.

206
00:16:55,138 --> 00:17:11,830
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, again, there's these ideas that by being in a particular posture, cross-legged, lotus position, back straight, spine erect, loosened, loose belly, all these posturings,

207
00:17:13,603 --> 00:17:18,465
[SPEAKER_01]: That doesn't do the work for you, but that is a... it's a game that you play to distract yourself.

208
00:17:18,505 --> 00:17:19,786
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a little ritual.

209
00:17:19,966 --> 00:17:24,889
[SPEAKER_02]: Little motion... motions you go through to create the significance of what you're about to do.

210
00:17:24,929 --> 00:17:34,073
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if you just sit down, stare at the wall or look down the hill and contemplate, why do you suffer when there is a... when you can't fulfill your desires?

211
00:17:34,133 --> 00:17:36,654
[SPEAKER_02]: Or why do you suffer when you experience something disagreeable?

212
00:17:37,495 --> 00:17:39,796
[SPEAKER_02]: Why do you suffer when you're paired with things you don't want?

213
00:17:39,916 --> 00:17:41,957
[SPEAKER_02]: Why do you suffer when you're separated from things you want?

214
00:17:43,486 --> 00:17:45,728
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, there's not much to it that you can perform.

215
00:17:47,030 --> 00:17:49,653
[SPEAKER_02]: And it's for many people, it's almost just abstract.

216
00:17:49,953 --> 00:17:59,323
[SPEAKER_02]: So, they need something more palpable, which is on a level of sort of mechanical performance, doing things with your body, particular posture, particular way of walking.

217
00:17:59,644 --> 00:18:02,346
[SPEAKER_02]: I must be stepping on the outsides of my soles or whatever.

218
00:18:02,366 --> 00:18:03,448
[SPEAKER_02]: I can't be walking like this.

219
00:18:03,488 --> 00:18:04,289
[SPEAKER_02]: I must walk slowly.

220
00:18:04,309 --> 00:18:05,029
[SPEAKER_02]: I can't walk fast.

221
00:18:06,249 --> 00:18:10,654
[SPEAKER_02]: All of that is being concerned with a particular, with a particular content of your perception.

222
00:18:10,774 --> 00:18:12,255
[SPEAKER_02]: Failing to see the nature behind it.

223
00:18:12,556 --> 00:18:19,803
[SPEAKER_02]: And certainly, failing to recognize the enduring feeling in regard to it.

224
00:18:19,903 --> 00:18:23,587
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess, athletic meditation.

225
00:18:23,988 --> 00:18:25,489
[SPEAKER_01]: How long do you sit?

226
00:18:25,790 --> 00:18:26,650
[SPEAKER_01]: Two hours?

227
00:18:27,131 --> 00:18:28,613
[SPEAKER_01]: Some guys can sit for two weeks.

228
00:18:29,193 --> 00:18:35,597
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, it's all based, as I said, it all revolves around trying to perceive, trying to catch, preying upon something.

229
00:18:36,157 --> 00:18:39,199
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why you would have to go through these specialized motions.

230
00:18:40,080 --> 00:18:42,821
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if you walk too fast, you can't be catching something.

231
00:18:42,881 --> 00:18:47,064
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you must walk slowly, slow down, stand, stand and just look at it.

232
00:18:47,104 --> 00:18:48,905
[SPEAKER_02]: Just, just, just grab it as it comes out.

233
00:18:49,525 --> 00:18:50,686
[SPEAKER_02]: Recognize it and that's it.

234
00:18:53,197 --> 00:18:56,840
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's nothing else than meditating with that same sensuality that you're trying to overcome.

235
00:18:57,080 --> 00:19:01,344
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if there's no... no unwholesome thoughts arising...

